 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Initial Primitives:** Under the standard 3DGS schedule, densification begins at iteration 500. Since $T_{mat}=200$, initial primitives (tagged $b_i=0$) have an age of 500, which is $\ge T_{mat}$. Thus, initial primitives are already mature at the first densification cycle [S1].
-*   **Early Densification Schedules:** If a training schedule densifies earlier than iteration 500 (e.g., at iteration 100), initial primitives would be immature ($100 - 0 < 200$). The tag $b_i$ is retained so AGE remains well-defined for such schedules [S1].
-*   **Cascade Prevention:** Without AGE, a Gaussian born at step $k$ can split at $k+1$, its child at $k+2$, causing an uncontrolled cascade. AGE ensures that with $T_{mat}=200$ and $\Delta_d=100$, a Gaussian can split or clone at most once every two densification cycles, converting an unbounded cascade into controlled refinement [S1].
